{"name":"Brooklyn Brownstone","hex":"#6d4b3f","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"brownstone","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A variety of brown to red-brown sandstone once popular as a building material.","A row house built of brownstone, especially in New York City."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To scour (steps) with a donkey stone."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/brownstone"}],"entries":[{"title":"Brooklyn","blocks":[{"text":"Brooklyn, coextensive with Kings County, is the most populous of the five boroughs and counties of New York City, New York. Located at the westernmost end of Long Island and formerly an independent city, Brooklyn shares a land border with the borough and county of Queens. It has several bridge and tunnel connections to the borough of Manhattan, across the East River, including the architecturally significant Brooklyn Bridge, and is connected to Staten Island by the Verrazzano–Narrows Bridge."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/Brooklyn_skyline.jpg/330px-Brooklyn_skyline.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brooklyn"},{"title":"Brownstone","blocks":[{"text":"Brownstone is a brown Triassic–Jurassic sandstone that was historically a popular building material. The term is also used in the United States and Canada to refer to a townhouse clad in this or any other aesthetically similar material."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/Harlem%2C_New_York_brownstones.jpg/330px-Harlem%2C_New_York_brownstones.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brownstone"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/Prospect_Park_boathouse_%2861446p%29.jpg/500px-Prospect_Park_boathouse_%2861446p%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail